 ## Subtopics
 
-### LLFF NDC Contraction
-A method to handle forward-facing scenes in the LLFF dataset by contracting the entire scene into NDC space for reconstruction and mesh extraction [S4]. It distinguishes itself by enabling watertight manifold reconstruction on unbounded scenes [S4]. Choose this when performing mesh extraction or using methods like DiffMC that require bounded NDC space [S4]. Parameters include a DiffMC resolution of 375 and 9x sample per-pixel SSAA [S4].
+### [LLFF Data Loading and Spiral Path Generation](11275-llff-data-loading-and-spiral-path-generation.md)
+This method prepares LLFF-style datasets for NeRF training by loading poses and images, applying geometric normalization, and generating a spiral render path for novel view synthesis. It differs from standard loading by explicitly handling the minification step via external tools and enforcing a specific train/test split protocol (every 8th image for testing).
+
+### [LLFF Dataset Preprocessing and Pose Normalization](14208-llff-dataset-preprocessing-and-pose-normalization.md)
+This method focuses on the geometric preprocessing of LLFF data, specifically correcting COLMAP axis conventions and normalizing scene scale for stable training. It ensures that the camera poses are in the correct coordinate system and that the scene depth is normalized to a standard range, facilitating consistent ray sampling.
+
+#### LLFF NDC Contraction
+A method to handle forward-facing scenes in the LLFF dataset by contracting the entire scene into NDC space for reconstruction and mesh extraction. It differs from standard loading by explicitly mapping the scene into NDC coordinates, which allows for uniform sampling in disparity space. It is chosen when mesh extraction or reconstruction in unbounded forward-facing scenes is required [S5].
+- **Parameters:** DiffMC resolution: 375; SSAA: 9x sample per-pixel [S5].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Loading and preprocessing is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of images. Ray generation and NDC conversion are $O(P)$ where $P$ is the number of pixels.
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ for storing rays and images.
-- **Measured Performance**: The LLFF dataset consists of 8 forward-facing scenes with varying resolutions. The NDC normalization ensures stable training across different scene scales [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Robust handling of COLMAP coordinate systems; automatic scene scaling ensures consistent training dynamics; NDC normalization simplifies sampling for forward-facing scenes [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Only suitable for forward-facing scenes; not applicable to 360-degree or indoor scenes with large depth variations [S4].
-- **When to Use**: When training NeRF on the LLFF dataset or similar forward-facing real-world captures [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use**: For 360-degree scenes, indoor scenes, or datasets with different coordinate conventions [S4].
+- **Time Complexity:** Loading is $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ for image I/O and ray generation. NDC conversion is $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ for storing rays and images.
+- **Performance:** Efficient for forward-facing scenes. NDC normalization improves sampling efficiency for distant objects [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Standardized preprocessing for LLFF; handles axis conversion automatically.
+- **Limitations:** Assumes forward-facing scenes; not suitable for 360-degree or bounded scenes without modification [S5].
 
 ## Background
-The LLFF (Local Light Field Fusion) dataset was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2019 as a benchmark for novel view synthesis of forward-facing real-world scenes [S4]. It is widely used in NeRF research due to its diverse set of scenes and realistic lighting conditions [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. The specific loading procedure, including axis correction and NDC normalization, was popularized by the original NeRF paper and subsequent implementations [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+The LLFF dataset (Local Light Field Fusion) was introduced by Mildenhall et al. [S5]. It consists of forward-facing scenes captured with a handheld camera. The dataset loading procedure described here is based on the implementation details from various NeRF repositories [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial] and the specific requirements for NDC-based reconstruction methods [S5].
 
